SUMMARY:Hidden Mythologies: Karelian Traditions & Myths
DESCRIPTION:Event Details:  Now in its second year\, the Hidden Mythologi
 eslecture series explores the rich and often overlooked mythological trad
 itions of the peoples of the Volga-Ural region and beyond. Through storie
 s of gods\, spirits\, legendary heroes\, sacred landscapes\, and traditio
 nal beliefs\, the series examines the ways mythology reflects and preserv
 es the cultures and worldviews of diverse communities across Eurasia.  Jo
 urney into the forests and mythological traditions of the Karelian people
 \, exploring the Meteliläinens and the celebrated Finnish-Karelian epic\
 , the Kalevala. Discover a world in which the forest\, poetry\, magic\, a
 nd mythology are deeply intertwined. The program features traditional Kar
 elian ballads\, including Vellamon Neidon Onginta\, Kanteleen Synty\, and
  Loitsu Saunassa. The program concludes with a live performance of the Ka
 relia folk song Kukkuu - Kukkuu.  Each program begins with a presentation
  by Dr. Golshat Safiullina exploring an aspect of the featured culture's 
 mythological traditions\, followed by Folk Song Club\, an interactive mus
 ical experience curated by musical director Daryana Antipova\, bringing a
 nother dimension to the stories\, beliefs\, and cultural heritage explore
 d in the lecture.  This event is held in collaboration with the Slavic an
 d East European Languages Department\, the Jacobs School of Music\, and t
 he CHAKCHAK: Languages and Cultures Foundation.Parking Information:  Limi
 ted complimentary parking is available in front of the Museum entrance (h
 ttps://maps.app.goo.gl/k4Txsojst2gSW5fUA) off Indiana Avenue. Free street
  parking is available in surrounding neighborhoods on weekends and weekda
 ys after 5:00 PM.  Paid parking is also available in the University Colle
 ctions at McCalla (https://maps.app.goo.gl/3i62KNcjDeCACruHA) parking lot
  off Indiana Avenue and the Indiana Memorial Union (https://maps.app.goo.
 gl/2n49VoLuF78o5twJA) visitor lot.  Metered street parking is available n
 ear Sample Gates and throughout downtown Bloomington\, including along Ki
 rkwood Avenue\, East 6th Street\, East 4th Street\, North Lincoln Street\
 , North Dunn Street\, and South Grant Street.Questions?  For accessibilit
 y assistance or questions about this event\, please reach out to Camden H
 ill at camrhill@iu.edu (mailto:camrhill@iu.edu) or (812) 855-1696.Note:  
 By attending this event\, you agree to be in photography that may be used
  by the University for promotional purposes. If you would not like to be 
 in any pictures\, please speak to a Museum staff member and they will inf
 orm assigned photographers of your request. If you see that your image ha
 s been used in error\, please reach out to iumaa@iu.edu (mailto:iumaa@iu.
 edu) to request that it be taken down.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Event Details:</strong></p><p>  N
 ow in its <strong>second year</strong>\, the <strong>Hidden Mythologies</
 strong><strong>lecture series</strong> explores the rich and often overlo
 oked mythological traditions of the peoples of the Volga-Ural region and 
 beyond. Through stories of gods\, spirits\, legendary heroes\, sacred lan
 dscapes\, and traditional beliefs\, the series examines the ways mytholog
 y reflects and preserves the cultures and worldviews of diverse communiti
 es across Eurasia.</p><p>  Journey into the forests and mythological trad
 itions of the <strong>Karelian people</strong>\, exploring the <strong>Me
 teliläinens</strong> and the celebrated Finnish-Karelian epic\, the <str
 ong><em>Kalevala</em></strong>. Discover a world in which the forest\, po
 etry\, magic\, and mythology are deeply intertwined. The program features
  traditional Karelian ballads\, including <strong><em>Vellamon Neidon Ong
 inta</em></strong><em>\, <strong>Kanteleen Synty\,</strong></em> and <str
 ong><em>Loitsu Saunassa</em></strong>. The program concludes with a live 
 performance of the <strong><em>Karelia folk song Kukkuu - Kukkuu</em></st
 rong>.</p><p>  Each program begins with a presentation by <strong>Dr. Gol
 shat Safiullina</strong> exploring an aspect of the featured culture's my
 thological traditions\, followed by Folk Song Club\, an interactive music
 al experience curated by musical director <strong>Daryana Antipova\,</str
 ong> bringing another dimension to the stories\, beliefs\, and cultural h
 eritage explored in the lecture.</p><p>  This event is held in collaborat
