SUMMARY:Hidden Mythologies: Altai Traditions & Myths
DESCRIPTION:Event Details:  Now in its second year\, the Hidden Mythologi
 eslecture series explores the rich and often overlooked mythological trad
 itions of the peoples of the Volga-Ural region and beyond. Through storie
 s of gods\, spirits\, legendary heroes\, sacred landscapes\, and traditio
 nal beliefs\, the series examines the ways mythology reflects and preserv
 es the cultures and worldviews of diverse communities across Eurasia.  In
  this installment\, discover the mythological and spiritual traditions of
  the Altai people\, including Ülgen\, Erlik\, Ak Ana\, Mergen\, and Bai 
 Ulgan. The lecture explores shamanism and the dualism of Altai cosmogony\
 , examining the powerful beings and opposing forces that shape the Altai 
 understanding of the universe. The program concludes with a live performa
 nce of the Altai folk song Kai Kozhon.  Each program begins with a presen
 tation by Dr. Golshat Safiullina exploring an aspect of the featured cult
 ure's mythological traditions\, followed by Folk Song Club\, an interacti
 ve musical experience curated by musical director Daryana Antipova\, brin
 ging another dimension to the stories\, beliefs\, and cultural heritage e
 xplored in the lecture.  This event is held in collaboration with the Sla
 vic and East European Languages Department\, the Jacobs School of Music\,
  and the CHAKCHAK: Languages and Cultures Foundation.Parking Information:
   Limited complimentary parking is available in front of the Museum entra
 nce (https://maps.app.goo.gl/k4Txsojst2gSW5fUA) off Indiana Avenue. Free 
 street parking is available in surrounding neighborhoods on weekends and 
 weekdays after 5:00 PM.  Paid parking is also available in the University
  Collections at McCalla (https://maps.app.goo.gl/3i62KNcjDeCACruHA) parki
 ng lot off Indiana Avenue and the Indiana Memorial Union (https://maps.ap
 p.goo.gl/2n49VoLuF78o5twJA) visitor lot.  Metered street parking is avail
 able near Sample Gates and throughout downtown Bloomington\, including al
 ong Kirkwood Avenue\, East 6th Street\, East 4th Street\, North Lincoln S
 treet\, North Dunn Street\, and South Grant Street.Questions?  For access
 ibility assistance or questions about this event\, please reach out to Ca
 mden Hill at camrhill@iu.edu (mailto:camrhill@iu.edu) or (812) 855-1696.N
 ote:  By attending this event\, you agree to be in photography that may b
 e used by the University for promotional purposes. If you would not like 
 to be in any pictures\, please speak to a Museum staff member and they wi
 ll inform assigned photographers of your request. If you see that your im
 age has been used in error\, please reach out to iumaa@iu.edu (mailto:ium
 aa@iu.edu) to request that it be taken down.
